<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">On Degrees in the Hasse Diagram
<br class="ltx_break">of the Strong Bruhat Order</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ron M. Adin <sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">§</sup>
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Mathematics and Statistics, Bar-Ilan University,
Ramat-Gan 52900, Israel. Email: <span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">radin@math.biu.ac.il</span></span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuval Roichman
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Mathematics and Statistics, Bar-Ilan University,
Ramat-Gan 52900, Israel. Email: <span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yuvalr@math.biu.ac.il</span></span></span></span><span id="id4" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Research of both authors was supported in part by
the Israel Science Foundation, founded by the Israel Academy of
Sciences and Humanities, and by the EC’s IHRP Programme, within the
Research Training Network “Algebraic Combinatorics in Europe”,
grant HPRN-CT-2001-00272.</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">March 12, 2006</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">For a permutation <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in the symmetric group <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> let the
<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">total degree</span> be its valency in the Hasse diagram of the strong Bruhat order on <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let the <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">down degree</span> be the number of permutations which are covered by <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in
the strong Bruhat order.
The maxima of
the total degree and the down degree and their values at a random permutation
are computed. Proofs involve variants of a classical theorem of Turán from
extremal graph theory.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>The Down, Up and Total Degrees</h2>

<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_df">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a permutation <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> let the
down degree <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of permutations in <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> which are
covered by <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in the strong Bruhat order. Let the up degree
<math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{+}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{+}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of permutations which cover <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in this order.
The total degree of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\pi):=d_{-}(\pi)+d_{+}(\pi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\pi):=d_{-}(\pi)+d_{+}(\pi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e., the valency of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hasse diagram of the strong
Bruhat order.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Explicitly, for <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a&lt;b\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a&lt;b\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a,b}=t_{b,a}\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a,b}=t_{b,a}\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be
the transposition interchanging <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>,
and for <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ell(\pi):=\min\{k\,|\,\pi=s_{i_{1}}s_{i_{2}}\cdots s_{i_{k}}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\pi):=\min\{k\,|\,\pi=s_{i_{1}}s_{i_{2}}\cdots s_{i_{k}}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">be the <span id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">length</span> of <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the standard Coxeter generators
<math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=t_{i,i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=t_{i,i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\leq i&lt;n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\leq i&lt;n)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\#\{t_{a,b}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)=\ell(\pi)-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\#\{t_{a,b}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)=\ell(\pi)-1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{+}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{+}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\#\{t_{a,b}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)=\ell(\pi)+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\#\{t_{a,b}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)=\ell(\pi)+1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d(\pi)=d_{-}(\pi)+d_{+}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d(\pi)=d_{-}(\pi)+d_{+}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\#\{t_{a,b}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)=\ell(\pi)\pm 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\#\{t_{a,b}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)=\ell(\pi)\pm 1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">For the general definitions and other properties of the weak and strong
Bruhat orders see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Ex. 3.75]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, §§2.1, 3.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We shall describe <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by its sequence of values
<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\pi(1),\ldots,\pi(n)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\pi(1),\ldots,\pi(n)]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_obs">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 1.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> covers <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in the strong Bruhat order on <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
there exist <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;k\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;k\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b:=\pi(i)&gt;\pi(k)=:a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b:=\pi(i)&gt;\pi(k)=:a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=t_{a,b}\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=t_{a,b}\pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, i.e.,
</span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\ldots,b,\ldots,a,\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\ldots,b,\ldots,a,\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and
</span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=[\ldots,a,\ldots,b,\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=[\ldots,a,\ldots,b,\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is no </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;\pi(j)&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;\pi(j)&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)=d_{-}(\pi^{-1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)=d_{-}(\pi^{-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}[123]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}[123]=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}[132]=d_{-}[213]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>132</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>213</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}[132]=d_{-}[213]=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}[321]=d_{-}[231]=d_{-}[312]=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>321</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>231</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>312</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}[321]=d_{-}[231]=d_{-}[312]=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand,
<math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d[321]=d[123]=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>321</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d[321]=d[123]=2</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d[213]=d[132]=d[312]=d[231]=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>213</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>132</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>312</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>231</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d[213]=d[132]=d[312]=d[231]=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The classical descent number of a permutation <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in the symmetric group
<math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of permutations in <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> which are covered by <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in the
(right) weak Bruhat order. Thus, the down degree may be considered as
a “strong descent number”.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_df">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}(\pi):=\{t_{a,b}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)=\ell(\pi)-1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}(\pi):=\{t_{a,b}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)=\ell(\pi)-1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the strong descent set of <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.7</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The strong descent set of <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[7,9,5,2,3,8,4,1,6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[7,9,5,2,3,8,4,1,6]</annotation></semantics></math>
is</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}(\pi)=\{t_{1,2},t_{1,3},t_{1,4},t_{2,5},t_{3,5},t_{4,5},t_{4,8},t_{5,7},t_{5,9},t_{6,7},t_{6,8},t_{8,9}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}(\pi)=\{t_{1,2},t_{1,3},t_{1,4},t_{2,5},t_{3,5},t_{4,5},t_{4,8},t_{5,7},t_{5,9},t_{6,7},t_{6,8},t_{8,9}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generalized pattern avoidance, involving strong descent sets, was applied by
Woo and Yong <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> to determine which Schubert varieties are Gorenstein.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_pro">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1.9</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The strong descent set <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> uniquely determines the permutation <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
By induction on <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The claim clearly holds for <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> be a permutation in <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}\in S_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}\in S_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be
the permutation obtained by deleting the value <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that, by Observation <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Observation 1.2 ‣ 1 The Down, Up and Total Degrees ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}({\bar{\pi}})=D_{-}(\pi)\setminus\{t_{a,n}\,|\,1\leq a&lt;n\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}({\bar{\pi}})=D_{-}(\pi)\setminus\{t_{a,n}\,|\,1\leq a&lt;n\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">By the induction hypothesis <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely determined by this set.
Hence it suffices to determine the position of <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now, if <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:=\pi^{-1}(n)&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:=\pi^{-1}(n)&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> then clearly <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\pi(j+1),n}\in D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\pi(j+1),n}\in D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, by Observation <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Observation 1.2 ‣ 1 The Down, Up and Total Degrees ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>,
<math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a,n}\in D_{-}(\pi)\Longrightarrow a\geq\pi(j+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a,n}\in D_{-}(\pi)\Longrightarrow a\geq\pi(j+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> determines</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}(j)=\pi(j+1)=\min\{a\,|\,t_{a,n}\in D_{-}(\pi)\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}(j)=\pi(j+1)=\min\{a\,|\,t_{a,n}\in D_{-}(\pi)\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and therefore determines <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that this set of <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>’s is empty if and only if <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=n</annotation></semantics></math>.
This completes the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Maximal Down Degree</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we compute the maximal value of the
down degree on <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and find all the permutations achieving the maximum.
We prove</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_pro">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every positive integer <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{d_{-}(\pi)|\ \pi\in S_{n}\}=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{d_{-}(\pi)|\ \pi\in S_{n}\}=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The same number appears as the order dimension of the strong Bruhat
poset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
An upper bound on the maximal down degree for finite Coxeter groups appears in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Prop. 3.4]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 2.1 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we need a classical theorem of Turán.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_df">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> be positive integers. The Turán graph
<math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{r}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{r}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-partite graph with <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and
all parts as equal in size as possible, i.e., each size is
either <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor n/r\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor n/r\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil n/r\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil n/r\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote by <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{r}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{r}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> the number of edges of <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{r}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{r}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, IV, Theorem 8]</cite>
<span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Turán’s Theorem)</span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every graph of order </span><math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with more than </span><math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{r}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{r}(n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> edges
contains a complete subgraph of order </span><math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{r}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{r}(n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is
the unique graph of order </span><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{r}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{r}(n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> edges that does not
contain a complete subgraph of order </span><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We shall apply
the special case <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math> (due to Mantel) of
Turán’s theorem to the following graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_df">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The strong descent graph of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>,
is the undirected graph whose set of vertices is <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and whose
set of edges is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{a,b\}\,|\,t_{a,b}\in D_{-}(\pi)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{a,b\}\,|\,t_{a,b}\in D_{-}(\pi)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By definition, the number of edges in <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Permutations for which the strong descent graph is connected are
called indecomposable. Their enumeration was studied
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, pp. 7–8]</cite>.
The number of components in <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the number of
global descents in <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi w_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi w_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}:=[n,n-1,\dots,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}:=[n,n-1,\dots,1]</annotation></semantics></math>),
which were introduced and studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Corollaries 6.3 and 6.4]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.7</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the strong descent graph <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is triangle-free.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Assume that <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> contains a triangle.
Then there exist <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a&lt;b&lt;c\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a&lt;b&lt;c\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a,b},t_{a,c},t_{b,c}\in D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a,b},t_{a,c},t_{b,c}\in D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Observation <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Observation 1.2 ‣ 1 The Down, Up and Total Degrees ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a,b},t_{b,c}\in D_{-}(\pi)\Longrightarrow\pi^{-1}(c)&lt;\pi^{-1}(b)&lt;\pi^{-1}(a)\Longrightarrow t_{a,c}\not\in D_{-}(\pi)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a,b},t_{b,c}\in D_{-}(\pi)\Longrightarrow\pi^{-1}(c)&lt;\pi^{-1}(b)&lt;\pi^{-1}(a)\Longrightarrow t_{a,c}\not\in D_{-}(\pi).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">This is a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 2.1 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</span>
By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 2.4 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>(1) together with Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, for every
<math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)\leq t_{2}(n)=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)\leq t_{2}(n)=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Equality holds since</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}([\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1,\lfloor n/2\rfloor+2,\dots,n,1,2,\dots,\lfloor n/2\rfloor])=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}([\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1,\lfloor n/2\rfloor+2,\dots,n,1,2,\dots,\lfloor n/2\rfloor])=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Next we classify (and enumerate) the permutations which achieve
the maximal down degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a permutation with maximal down degree. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> has no decreasing subsequence of length 4.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Assume that <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\ldots d\ldots c\ldots b\ldots a\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\ldots d\ldots c\ldots b\ldots a\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;c&gt;b&gt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;c&gt;b&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(a)-\pi^{-1}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(a)-\pi^{-1}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> minimal. Then
<math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a,b},t_{b,c},t_{c,d}\in D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a,b},t_{b,c},t_{c,d}\in D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> but,
by Observation <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Observation 1.2 ‣ 1 The Down, Up and Total Degrees ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a,d}\not\in D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a,d}\not\in D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is not a complete bipartite graph,
since <math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,b\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b,c\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> are edges but <math id="S2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> is not.
By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, combined with Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 2.4 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>(2),
the number of edges in <math id="S2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is less than <math id="S2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_pro">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.9</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every positive integer <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\{\pi\in S_{n}\,|\,d_{-}(\pi)=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor\}=\cases{n,&amp;if $n$ is odd;\cr n/2,&amp;if $n$ is even.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> is odd;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> is even.</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\{\pi\in S_{n}\,|\,d_{-}(\pi)=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor\}=\cases{n,&amp;if $n$ is odd;\cr n/2,&amp;if $n$ is even.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Each such permutation has the form</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[t+m+1,t+m+2,\ldots,n,t+1,t+2,\ldots,t+m,1,2,\ldots,t]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[t+m+1,t+m+2,\ldots,n,t+1,t+2,\ldots,t+m,1,2,\ldots,t],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq t\leq n-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq t\leq n-m</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=n-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=n-m</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>) gives the same permutation as <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-m</annotation></semantics></math>
instead of <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
It is easy to verify the claim for <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 2.4 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>(2), <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to
the complete bipartite graph <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, each side of the graph contains at least two vertices.
Let <math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> be two vertices on one side, and <math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math> two vertices on the
other side of the graph.
Since <math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b,c},t_{b,d}\in D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b,c},t_{b,d}\in D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>, there are three possible cases:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;c</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\ldots c\ldots d\ldots b\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\ldots c\ldots d\ldots b\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math>
(since <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\ldots d\ldots c\ldots b\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\ldots d\ldots c\ldots b\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math> contradicts
<math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b,d}\in D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b,d}\in D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;b&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;b&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\ldots d\ldots b\ldots c\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\ldots d\ldots b\ldots c\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\ldots b\ldots c\ldots d\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\ldots b\ldots c\ldots d\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math>
(since <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\ldots b\ldots d\ldots c\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\ldots b\ldots d\ldots c\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math> contradicts
<math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b,c}\in D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b,c}\in D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">The same also holds for <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, but then
cases <math id="S2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> are impossible since <math id="S2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1&lt;c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1&lt;c</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus necessarily <math id="S2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> appears before <math id="S2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and case <math id="S2.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore
impossible for <span id="S2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> <math id="S2.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> on the same side as <math id="S2.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words: no vertex on the same side as <math id="S2.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math> is intermediate,
either in position (in <math id="S2.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>) or in value, to <math id="S2.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Assume now that <math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
The vertices not on the side of <math id="S2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> form (in <math id="S2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>) a block of length <math id="S2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/2</annotation></semantics></math>
of numbers which are consecutive in value as well in position.
They also form an increasing subsequence of <math id="S2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>
is bipartite.
The numbers preceding them are all larger in value, and are increasing;
the numbers succeeding them are all smaller in value, are increasing,
and contain <math id="S2.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to check that each permutation <math id="S2.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> of this form
has maximal <math id="S2.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="S2.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is completely determined by
the length <math id="S2.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq t\leq n/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq t\leq n/2</annotation></semantics></math> of the last increasing subsequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> odd one obtains a similar classification, except that the length
of the side not containing <math id="S2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor n/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor n/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil n/2\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil n/2\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Maximal Total Degree</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Obviously, the maximal value of the total degree <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=d_{-}+d_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=d_{-}+d_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>
cannot exceed <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n\choose 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n\choose 2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the total number of transpositions in <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is slightly better than the bound <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>
obtainable from Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 2.1 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.
The actual maximal value is smaller.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>,
the maximal total degree in the Hasse diagram of the
strong Bruhat order on <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to prove this result, associate with each permutation <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
a graph <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>, whose set of vertices is <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and
whose set of edges is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{a,b\}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)-\ell(\pi)=\pm 1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{a,b\}\,|\,\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)-\ell(\pi)=\pm 1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This graph has many properties; e.g., it is <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>-free and is the
edge-disjoint union of two triangle-free graphs on the same set of vertices.
However, these properties are not strong enough to imply the above result.
A property which does imply it is the following bound on the minimal degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a vertex in <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> with degree at most <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Assume, on the contrary, that each vertex in <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> has at least
<math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor n/2\rfloor+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor n/2\rfloor+2</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors. This applies, in particular, to the vertex
<math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Being the first value of <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, the neighborhood of <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>,
viewed as a subsequence of <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\pi(2),\ldots,\pi(n)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\pi(2),\ldots,\pi(n)]</annotation></semantics></math>,
consists of a shuffle of a decreasing sequence of numbers larger than <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>
and an increasing sequence of numbers smaller than <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> be the rightmost neighbor of <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The intersection of the neighborhood of <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> with
the neighborhood of <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is of cardinality at most two.
Thus the degree of <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is at most</p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-(\lfloor n/2\rfloor+2)+2=\lceil n/2\rceil\leq\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-(\lfloor n/2\rfloor+2)+2=\lceil n/2\rceil\leq\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1 ‣ 3 Maximal Total Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</span>
First note that, by definition, the total degree of <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hasse diagram
of the strong Bruhat order is equal to the number of edges in <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will prove that this number <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\Gamma(\pi))\leq\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\Gamma(\pi))\leq\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2</annotation></semantics></math>,
by induction on <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The claim is clearly true for <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that the claim holds for <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> be a vertex of <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> with minimal degree,
and let <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}\in S_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}\in S_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the permutation obtained from <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> by
deleting the value <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>
(and decreasing by <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> all the values larger than <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>). Then</p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\Gamma({\bar{\pi}}))\geq e(\Gamma(\pi)\setminus a)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\Gamma({\bar{\pi}}))\geq e(\Gamma(\pi)\setminus a),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where the latter is the number of edges in <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> which are not
incident with the vertex <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the induction hypothesis and Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2 ‣ 3 Maximal Total Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e(\Gamma(\pi))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e(\Gamma(\pi))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e(\Gamma(\pi)\setminus a)+d(a)\leq e(\Gamma({\bar{\pi}}))+d(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e(\Gamma(\pi)\setminus a)+d(a)\leq e(\Gamma({\bar{\pi}}))+d(a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lfloor(n-1)^{2}/4\rfloor+(n-1)-2+\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lfloor(n-1)^{2}/4\rfloor+(n-1)-2+\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Equality holds since, letting <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\lfloor n/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\lfloor n/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\Gamma([m+1,m+2,\ldots,n,1,2,\dots,m]))=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\Gamma([m+1,m+2,\ldots,n,1,2,\dots,m]))=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\{\pi\in S_{n}\,|\,d(\pi)=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2\}=\cases{2,&amp;if $n=2$;\cr 4,&amp;if $n=3$ or $n=4$;\cr 8,&amp;if $n\geq 6$ is even;\cr 16,&amp;if $n\geq 5$ is odd.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> or </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> is even;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> is odd.</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\{\pi\in S_{n}\,|\,d(\pi)=\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor+n-2\}=\cases{2,&amp;if $n=2$;\cr 4,&amp;if $n=3$ or $n=4$;\cr 8,&amp;if $n\geq 6$ is even;\cr 16,&amp;if $n\geq 5$ is odd.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The extremal permutations have one of the following forms:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}:=[m+1,m+2,\ldots,n,1,2,\ldots,m]\qquad(m\in\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil\})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}:=[m+1,m+2,\ldots,n,1,2,\ldots,m]\qquad(m\in\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil\}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the permutations obtained from <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by one or more of the following operations:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{r}:=[\pi(n),\pi(n-1),\ldots,\pi(2),\pi(1)]\qquad\mbox{\rm(reversing $\pi$),}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>(reversing </mtext><mi>π</mi><mtext>),</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{r}:=[\pi(n),\pi(n-1),\ldots,\pi(2),\pi(1)]\qquad\mbox{\rm(reversing $\pi$),}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{s}:=\pi\cdot t_{1,n}\qquad\mbox{\rm(interchanging $\pi(1)$ and $\pi(n)$),}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>(interchanging </mtext><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext>),</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{s}:=\pi\cdot t_{1,n}\qquad\mbox{\rm(interchanging $\pi(1)$ and $\pi(n)$),}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{t}:=t_{1,n}\cdot\pi\qquad\mbox{\rm(interchanging $1$ and $n$ in $\pi$).}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>(interchanging </mtext><mn>1</mn><mtext> and </mtext><mi>n</mi><mtext> in </mtext><mi>π</mi><mtext>).</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{t}:=t_{1,n}\cdot\pi\qquad\mbox{\rm(interchanging $1$ and $n$ in $\pi$).}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
It is not difficult to see that all the specified permutations are indeed
extremal, and their number is as claimed (for all <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The claim that there are no other extremal permutations will be proved
by induction on <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
For small values of <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (say <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>) this may be verified directly.
Assume now that the claim holds for some <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> be extremal.
Following the proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2 ‣ 3 Maximal Total Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
let <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> be a vertex of <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> with degree at most
<math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is either <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math> or its rightmost neighbor.
As in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1 ‣ 3 Maximal Total Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>,
let <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the permutation obtained from <math id="S3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> by
deleting the value <math id="S3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> (and decreasing by <math id="S3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> all the values larger than <math id="S3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>).
All the inequalities in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1 ‣ 3 Maximal Total Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> must hold as
equalities, namely:
<math id="S3.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\Gamma(\pi)\setminus a)=e(\Gamma({\bar{\pi}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\Gamma(\pi)\setminus a)=e(\Gamma({\bar{\pi}}))</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(a)=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(a)=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S3.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}</annotation></semantics></math> is extremal in <math id="S3.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the induction hypothesis, <math id="S3.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}</annotation></semantics></math> must have one of the prescribed forms.
In all of them, <math id="S3.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bar{\pi}}(1),{\bar{\pi}}(n)\}=\{m,m+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bar{\pi}}(1),{\bar{\pi}}(n)\}=\{m,m+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge of <math id="S3.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma({\bar{\pi}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma({\bar{\pi}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore the corresponding edge <math id="S3.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pi(1),\pi(n+1)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi(1),\pi(n+1)\}</annotation></semantics></math>
(or <math id="S3.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pi(2),\pi(n+1)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi(2),\pi(n+1)\}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S3.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pi(1),\pi(n)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi(1),\pi(n)\}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>)
is an edge of <math id="S3.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)\setminus a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)\setminus a</annotation></semantics></math>, namely of <math id="S3.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.p9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq\pi(1),\pi(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq\pi(1),\pi(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.p9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the rightmost neighbor of <math id="S3.p9.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
contradicting the choice of <math id="S3.p9.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.p9.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> we may use the operation <math id="S3.p9.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\mapsto\pi^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\mapsto\pi^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus we may assume from now on that <math id="S3.p9.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(a)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of neighbors of <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume first that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}=\pi_{0}=[m+1,m+2,\ldots,n,1,2,\ldots,m]\qquad(m\in\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil\})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}=\pi_{0}=[m+1,m+2,\ldots,n,1,2,\ldots,m]\qquad(m\in\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil\}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">Noting that <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil n/2\rceil=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil n/2\rceil=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and
keeping in mind the decrease in certain values during the transition
<math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\mapsto{\bar{\pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\mapsto{\bar{\pi}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following cases:</p>
<dl id="S3.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item"></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;m+1:" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;m+1:</annotation></semantics></math>
in this case <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\ldots,m\not\in N(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\ldots,m\not\in N(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(a)\leq n-m\leq\lceil n/2\rceil=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor&lt;\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(a)\leq n-m\leq\lceil n/2\rceil=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor&lt;\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is not extremal.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item"></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(2) <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;m:" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;m:</annotation></semantics></math>
in this case <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+3,\ldots,n+1,m+1\not\in N(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+3,\ldots,n+1,m+1\not\in N(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(a)\leq 1+(m-1)\leq\lceil n/2\rceil&lt;\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(a)\leq 1+(m-1)\leq\lceil n/2\rceil&lt;\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Again, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is not extremal.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item"></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(3) <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\{m,m+1\}:" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\{m,m+1\}:</annotation></semantics></math>
in this case</p>
<table id="S3.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(a)=1+m\leq\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(a)=1+m\leq\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor+1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with equality iff <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.
This gives <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the required form (either <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">A similar analysis for <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}=\pi_{0}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}=\pi_{0}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> gives extremal permutations
only for <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\{m+1,m+2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\{m+1,m+2\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(a)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(a)=3</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}=[2413]\in S_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}=[2413]\in S_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The permutations obtained are <math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[32514]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>32514</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[32514]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[42513]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>42513</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[42513]</annotation></semantics></math>, which are
<math id="S3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}^{rt},\pi_{0}^{rst}\in S_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}^{rt},\pi_{0}^{rst}\in S_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The other possible values of <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained by the <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\mapsto\pi^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\mapsto\pi^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\mapsto\pi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\mapsto\pi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> operations from the ones above,
and yield analogous results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Expectation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we prove an exact formula for the expectation of
the down degree of a permutation in <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every positive integer <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the expected down degree of a random permutation in <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\pi\in S_{n}}[d_{-}(\pi)]=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\sum_{j=2}^{i}\sum_{k=2}^{j}\frac{1}{i\cdot(k-1)}=(n+1)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{i}-2n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\pi\in S_{n}}[d_{-}(\pi)]=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\sum_{j=2}^{i}\sum_{k=2}^{j}\frac{1}{i\cdot(k-1)}=(n+1)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{i}-2n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It follows that</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\pi\in S_{n}}[d_{-}(\pi)]=n\ln n+O(n)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\pi\in S_{n}}[d_{-}(\pi)]=n\ln n+O(n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\pi\in S_{n}}[d(\pi)]=2n\ln n+O(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\pi\in S_{n}}[d(\pi)]=2n\ln n+O(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To prove Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1 ‣ 4 Expectation ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> we need some notation.
For <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the permutation
obtained from <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> by omitting all letters which are larger than
or equal to <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, if <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[6,1,4,8,3,2,5,9,7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[6,1,4,8,3,2,5,9,7]</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|9}=[6,1,4,8,3,2,5,7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|9}=[6,1,4,8,3,2,5,7]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|7}=[6,1,4,3,2,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|7}=[6,1,4,3,2,5]</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|4}=[1,3,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|4}=[1,3,2]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Also, denote by <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{|j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{|j}</annotation></semantics></math> the suffix of length <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, if <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[6,1,4,8,3,2,5,9,7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[6,1,4,8,3,2,5,9,7]</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{|3}=[5,9,7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{|3}=[5,9,7]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|4}^{|2}=[3,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|4}^{|2}=[3,2]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l.t.r.m.(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l.t.r.m.(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of left-to-right maxima in <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l.t.r.m.(\pi):=\#\{i\,|\,\pi(i)=\max_{1\leq j\leq i}\pi(j)\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l.t.r.m.(\pi):=\#\{i\,|\,\pi(i)=\max_{1\leq j\leq i}\pi(j)\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|{i+1}}^{-1}(i)=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|{i+1}}^{-1}(i)=j</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{-}(\pi_{|i+1})-d_{-}(\pi_{|i})=l.t.r.m.(\pi_{|i}^{|i-j})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi_{|i+1})-d_{-}(\pi_{|i})=l.t.r.m.(\pi_{|i}^{|i-j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1 ‣ 4 Expectation ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</span>
Clearly, for every <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S4.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\left[d_{-}(\pi_{|i+1})-d_{-}(\pi_{|i})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\left[d_{-}(\pi_{|i+1})-d_{-}(\pi_{|i})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, by Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3 ‣ 4 Expectation ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)=\sum_{i=2}^{n}l.t.r.m.(\pi_{|i}^{|i-j_{i}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)=\sum_{i=2}^{n}l.t.r.m.(\pi_{|i}^{|i-j_{i}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the position of <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|{i+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|{i+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{i}:=\pi_{|{i+1}}^{-1}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}:=\pi_{|{i+1}}^{-1}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Define a random variable <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to be the down degree <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>
of a random (uniformly distributed) permutation <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for each <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random (uniformly distributed) permutation in <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and therefore
<math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j=\pi_{|i+1}^{-1}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=\pi_{|i+1}^{-1}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly distributed in <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,i\}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|i+1}^{|i-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|i+1}^{|i-j}</annotation></semantics></math> is essentially a random (uniformly distributed)
permutation in <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i-j}</annotation></semantics></math> (after monotonically renaming its values).
Therefore, by linearity of the expectation,</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[X]=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i}\sum_{j=1}^{i}E[X_{i-j}]=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i}\sum_{t=0}^{i-1}E[X_{t}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[X]=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i}\sum_{j=1}^{i}E[X_{i-j}]=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i}\sum_{t=0}^{i-1}E[X_{t}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}:=l.t.r.m.(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}:=l.t.r.m.(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> for a random <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Corollary 1.3.8]</cite> that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\sigma\in S_{t}}q^{l.t.r.m.(\sigma)}=\prod_{k=1}^{t}(q+k-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\sigma\in S_{t}}q^{l.t.r.m.(\sigma)}=\prod_{k=1}^{t}(q+k-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">It follows that, for <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E[X_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E[X_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{t!}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{t}}l.t.r.m.(\sigma)=\frac{1}{t!}\left.\left(\frac{d}{dq}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{t}}q^{l.t.r.m.(\sigma)}\right)\right|_{q=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{t!}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{t}}l.t.r.m.(\sigma)=\frac{1}{t!}\left.\left(\frac{d}{dq}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{t}}q^{l.t.r.m.(\sigma)}\right)\right|_{q=1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{t!}\left.\left(\frac{d}{dq}\prod_{k=1}^{t}(q+k-1)\right)\right|_{q=1}=\frac{1}{t!}\sum_{r=1}^{t}\prod_{{1\leq k\leq t}\atop k\neq r}k=\sum\limits_{r=1}^{t}\frac{1}{r}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></munder></mstyle><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{t!}\left.\left(\frac{d}{dq}\prod_{k=1}^{t}(q+k-1)\right)\right|_{q=1}=\frac{1}{t!}\sum_{r=1}^{t}\prod_{{1\leq k\leq t}\atop k\neq r}k=\sum\limits_{r=1}^{t}\frac{1}{r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">Of course, <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[X_{0}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[X_{0}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Substituting these values into (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4 Expectation ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) gives</p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[X]=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\sum_{t=1}^{i-1}\sum_{r=1}^{t}\frac{1}{i\cdot r}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[X]=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\sum_{t=1}^{i-1}\sum_{r=1}^{t}\frac{1}{i\cdot r}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.4" class="ltx_p">and this is equivalent (with <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=t+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=r+1</annotation></semantics></math>) to the first formula
in the statement of the theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The second formula may be obtained through the following manipulations:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E[X]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=2}^{n}\sum_{j=2}^{i}\sum_{k=2}^{j}\frac{1}{i\cdot(k-1)}=\sum_{2\leq k\leq j\leq i\leq n}\frac{1}{i\cdot(k-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=2}^{n}\sum_{j=2}^{i}\sum_{k=2}^{j}\frac{1}{i\cdot(k-1)}=\sum_{2\leq k\leq j\leq i\leq n}\frac{1}{i\cdot(k-1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{2\leq k\leq i\leq n}\frac{i-k+1}{i\cdot(k-1)}=\sum_{2\leq k\leq i\leq n}\left(\frac{1}{k-1}-\frac{1}{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{2\leq k\leq i\leq n}\frac{i-k+1}{i\cdot(k-1)}=\sum_{2\leq k\leq i\leq n}\left(\frac{1}{k-1}-\frac{1}{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{2\leq k\leq n}\frac{n-k+1}{k-1}-\sum_{2\leq i\leq n}\frac{i-1}{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{2\leq k\leq n}\frac{n-k+1}{k-1}-\sum_{2\leq i\leq n}\frac{i-1}{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k-1}-(n-1)-(n-1)+\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k-1}-(n-1)-(n-1)+\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{i}-2n+\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{i}-2n+\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Corollary 4.2 ‣ 4 Expectation ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</span> Notice that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{i}=\ln n+O(1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{i}=\ln n+O(1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.2" class="ltx_p">(The next term in the asymptotic expansion is Euler’s constant.)
Substitute into Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1 ‣ 4 Expectation ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> to obtain the desired result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Generalized Down Degrees</h2>

<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_df">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq r&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq r&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> let</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi):=\{t_{a,b}|\ \ell(\pi)&gt;\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)&gt;\ell(\pi)-2r\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi):=\{t_{a,b}|\ \ell(\pi)&gt;\ell(t_{a,b}\pi)&gt;\ell(\pi)-2r\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-th strong descent set of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-th
down degree as</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi):=\#D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi):=\#D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The first strong descent set and
down degree are those studied in the previous section; namely,
<math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}^{(1)}(\pi)=D_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}^{(1)}(\pi)=D_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}^{(1)}(\pi)=d_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}^{(1)}(\pi)=d_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th strong descent set is the set of inversions:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}^{(n-1)}(\pi)=\{t_{a,b}\,|\,a&lt;b,\,\pi^{-1}(a)&gt;\pi^{-1}(b)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}^{(n-1)}(\pi)=\{t_{a,b}\,|\,a&lt;b,\,\pi^{-1}(a)&gt;\pi^{-1}(b)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}^{(n-1)}(\pi)={\it inv}(\pi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑖𝑛𝑣</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}^{(n-1)}(\pi)={\it inv}(\pi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the inversion number of <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_obs">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 5.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a&lt;b\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a&lt;b\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a,b}\in D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a,b}\in D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\dots,b,\dots,a,\dots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\dots,b,\dots,a,\dots]</annotation></semantics></math> and
there are less than <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> letters between the positions of <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> whose value is between <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[7,9,5,2,3,8,4,1,6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[7,9,5,2,3,8,4,1,6]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}^{(1)}(\pi)=\{t_{6,7},t_{6,8},t_{1,4},t_{1,3},t_{1,2},t_{4,8},t_{4,5},t_{8,9},t_{3,5},t_{2,5},t_{5,9},t_{5,7}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}^{(1)}(\pi)=\{t_{6,7},t_{6,8},t_{1,4},t_{1,3},t_{1,2},t_{4,8},t_{4,5},t_{8,9},t_{3,5},t_{2,5},t_{5,9},t_{5,7}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{-}^{(2)}(\pi)=D_{-}^{(1)}(\pi)\cup\{t_{6,9},t_{1,8},t_{4,9},t_{4,7},t_{3,9},t_{3,7},t_{2,9},t_{2,7}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{-}^{(2)}(\pi)=D_{-}^{(1)}(\pi)\cup\{t_{6,9},t_{1,8},t_{4,9},t_{4,7},t_{3,9},t_{3,7},t_{2,9},t_{2,7}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq r&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq r&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)=d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi^{-1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)=d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi^{-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> By Observation <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Observation 5.3 ‣ 5 Generalized Down Degrees ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a,b}\in D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a,b}\in D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\pi^{-1}(a),\pi^{-1}(b)}\in D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\pi^{-1}(a),\pi^{-1}(b)}\in D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_df">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-th strong descent graph of <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>,
is the graph whose set of vertices is <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and whose
set of edges is</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{a,b\}|\ t_{a,b}\in D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{a,b\}|\ t_{a,b}\in D_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma generalizes Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Maximal Down Degree ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.7</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>
contains no subgraph isomorphic to the complete graph <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{r+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{r+2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Assume that there is a subgraph in
<math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> isomorphic to <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{r+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{r+2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there exists a decreasing subsequence</p>
<table id="S5.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq a_{1}&gt;a_{2}&gt;\cdots&gt;a_{r+2}\geq 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq a_{1}&gt;a_{2}&gt;\cdots&gt;a_{r+2}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">such that for all <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq r+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq r+2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a_{i},a_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a_{i},a_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-th
strong descents of <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, for every <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;r+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;r+2</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a_{i},a_{i+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a_{i},a_{i+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-th strong descents of <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. This
implies that, for every <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;r+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;r+2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> appears to the right of
<math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by Observation <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Observation 5.3 ‣ 5 Generalized Down Degrees ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, <math id="S5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a_{1},a_{r+2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a_{1},a_{r+2}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is not an <math id="S5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-th strong descent. Contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq r&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq r&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="max\{d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)\,|\,\pi\in S_{n}\}\leq t_{r+1}(n)\leq{r+1\choose 2}\left(\frac{n}{r+1}\right)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">max\{d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)\,|\,\pi\in S_{n}\}\leq t_{r+1}(n)\leq{r+1\choose 2}\left(\frac{n}{r+1}\right)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Combining Turán’s Theorem together with Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 5.7 ‣ 5 Generalized Down Degrees ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=n-1</annotation></semantics></math> equality holds in
Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 5.8 ‣ 5 Generalized Down Degrees ‣ On Degrees in the Hasse Diagramof the Strong Bruhat Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.9</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the permutation
obtained from <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> by omitting the value <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is the position of <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)-d_{-}^{(r)}({\bar{\pi}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)-d_{-}^{(r)}({\bar{\pi}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">equals the number of <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th almost left-to-right minima in the <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th
suffix of <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\pi}}</annotation></semantics></math>, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. This observation may be applied to
calculate the expectation of <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}^{(r)}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements.</span>
The concept of strong descent graph came up during conversations with
Francesco Brenti. Its name and certain other improvements were suggested
by Christian Krattenthaler. Thanks also to Nathan Reading, Amitai Regev,
Alexander Yong, and the anonymous referees.</p>
</div>
</section>
